WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.458 --> 00:00:15.541
My name's Sam Dracott. I'm a farrier,

00:00:15.541 --> 00:00:17.583
and I'm going to show
you the cleaning process

00:00:17.583 --> 00:00:20.583
and the shoeing process of a horse.

00:00:21.666 --> 00:00:25.000
The main thing I'll do
is riding in schools.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:26.416
There's a lot more science behind it

00:00:26.416 --> 00:00:27.625
than meets the eye.

00:00:27.625 --> 00:00:30.041
So what you would need is an anatomical

00:00:30.041 --> 00:00:32.125
sort of understanding of the horse's foot,

00:00:32.125 --> 00:00:34.583
with tendons, ligaments, arteries, veins,

00:00:34.583 --> 00:00:36.916
bones, columns, how they grow.

00:00:36.916 --> 00:00:39.000
Also, we need to have
some blacksmithing skills

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.500
in order to construct the shoe.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:43.125
Anything that needs to be done

00:00:43.125 --> 00:00:46.041
to help maintain the
horse's life span, really.

00:00:48.458 --> 00:00:52.208
So, the actual condition of
this foot is pretty good.

00:00:52.208 --> 00:00:55.625
It's just been six weeks,
and it's just grown.

00:00:55.625 --> 00:00:57.791
So, that triangle bit in the middle

00:00:57.791 --> 00:00:59.666
is known as the frog.

00:00:59.666 --> 00:01:02.083
This is known for helping grip

00:01:02.083 --> 00:01:04.791
and aiding blood supply around the foot.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:09.041
So, I've got my straight blade,

00:01:09.041 --> 00:01:10.250
and I'm actually cleaning out the sole.

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:12.666
As you can see, it's sort
of going to crumble out,

00:01:12.666 --> 00:01:15.166
and then this will help me, aiding in

00:01:15.166 --> 00:01:17.041
how much foot this horse
has actually grown.

00:01:17.041 --> 00:01:18.500
And I'll just trim it all back.

00:01:20.666 --> 00:01:21.500
As the hoof grows,

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:23.041
it's almost like a giant fingernail.

00:01:23.041 --> 00:01:24.875
So it's always, constantly growing.

00:01:24.875 --> 00:01:27.125
So you need to trim that back,

00:01:27.125 --> 00:01:29.291
reset the shoe back,

00:01:29.291 --> 00:01:31.500
so the bone column of
the structure of the leg

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:33.125
is actually set in the correct place,

00:01:33.125 --> 00:01:34.958
rather than, if it's too long,

00:01:34.958 --> 00:01:37.708
it will be under a lot of
pressure for the tendons.

00:01:37.708 --> 00:01:39.125
So, imagine always wearing

00:01:39.125 --> 00:01:40.958
a huge pair of shoes for yourself.

00:01:40.958 --> 00:01:42.458
It makes it very
difficult for you to walk.

00:01:42.458 --> 00:01:44.666
So to have the horses regularly trimmed

00:01:44.666 --> 00:01:46.791
will help to actually
maintain the life span

00:01:46.791 --> 00:01:48.583
of the horse and the function of it

00:01:48.583 --> 00:01:50.291
doing whatever discipline it's in.

00:01:51.791 --> 00:01:53.375
As you take the sole out,

00:01:53.375 --> 00:01:55.583
it will start to become slightly waxy.

00:01:55.583 --> 00:01:57.541
It won't crumble out quite so easy,

00:01:57.541 --> 00:01:58.875
and that's a good indication

00:01:58.875 --> 00:02:00.833
you're getting as far as you should go.

00:02:00.833 --> 00:02:02.166
And then you should be able to just trim

00:02:02.166 --> 00:02:04.291
that bit off of the excess
hoof, and that's it.

00:02:04.291 --> 00:02:06.416
So you want to trim from there, really.

00:02:08.375 --> 00:02:11.208
So I'd use these giant pair
of toenail cutters, really,

00:02:11.208 --> 00:02:13.250
and you'd just start
trimming the foot off,

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:15.250
nice and level, as flat as you can.

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:16.375
The bit I'm actually trimming

00:02:16.375 --> 00:02:19.208
is exactly what a toenail is made out of.

00:02:19.208 --> 00:02:20.416
So it's millions and millions

00:02:20.416 --> 00:02:23.708
of strands of hair,
actually, bonded together.

00:02:23.708 --> 00:02:26.541
So it's exactly the same keratin protein

00:02:26.541 --> 00:02:28.291
as a fingernail would have.

00:02:33.541 --> 00:02:35.416
There is actually no nerve endings

00:02:35.416 --> 00:02:37.000
on the bits that I touch.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:38.625
The horse feels the sensation.

00:02:38.625 --> 00:02:40.458
So imagine having a shoe on

00:02:40.458 --> 00:02:43.041
and somebody taps the bottom of your shoe.

00:02:43.041 --> 00:02:44.750
You would feel the sensation of it,

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:45.958
but it doesn't actually hurt you.

00:02:45.958 --> 00:02:47.875
It's pretty much exactly the same.

00:02:51.708 --> 00:02:53.208
The giant nail file is actually

00:02:53.208 --> 00:02:54.333
what we call a rasp.

00:02:54.333 --> 00:02:55.833
And then you would use the rasp

00:02:55.833 --> 00:02:56.916
to get it nice and flat,

00:02:56.916 --> 00:02:58.250
because these horses actually walk

00:02:58.250 --> 00:02:59.875
on their fingernails, unlike us.

00:02:59.875 --> 00:03:02.291
So this has to be flat to help them

00:03:02.291 --> 00:03:03.625
walk evenly on their joints.

00:03:03.625 --> 00:03:06.000
'Cause if you did one side
higher than the other,

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:07.291
then they'd be walking unevenly,

00:03:07.291 --> 00:03:08.833
and it would put excessive strain

00:03:08.833 --> 00:03:10.208
on their joints and the tendons.

00:03:10.208 --> 00:03:12.833
So the idea is to get
it as flat as you can.

00:03:12.833 --> 00:03:14.333
So almost perfect.

00:03:19.041 --> 00:03:21.833
So, say if it wasn't
quite 100% perfectly flat,

00:03:21.833 --> 00:03:24.791
the shoe would actually
finish that job for you.

00:03:24.791 --> 00:03:28.541
So, every foot that I
do is completely unique.

00:03:28.541 --> 00:03:30.791
There's no feet that two are the same.

00:03:30.791 --> 00:03:32.833
I buy these sort of factory made,

00:03:32.833 --> 00:03:34.208
and to a standard size,

00:03:34.208 --> 00:03:36.500
and then we put them in
the forge to heat them up

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:38.375
to make them flexible, malleable enough

00:03:38.375 --> 00:03:40.791
to make them custom for the horse.

00:03:40.791 --> 00:03:42.333
So the process would start

00:03:42.333 --> 00:03:44.583
with getting the right size of the horse,

00:03:44.583 --> 00:03:47.041
sort of by memory and a lot of practice.

00:03:47.041 --> 00:03:48.208
As you are trimming the feet,

00:03:48.208 --> 00:03:49.791
you sort of see the shape

00:03:49.791 --> 00:03:51.250
that the horse's feet have.

00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:53.916
So you might see it's
slightly straight on one side,

00:03:53.916 --> 00:03:55.000
quite flare on one side.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:56.750
You would clock that in your brain,

00:03:56.750 --> 00:03:58.541
automatically get a rough size

00:03:58.541 --> 00:03:59.833
and shape that you would need,

00:03:59.833 --> 00:04:01.625
and nine times out of 10,

00:04:01.625 --> 00:04:03.916
I would get it pretty much
bang on straight away.

00:04:06.708 --> 00:04:08.375
I'm now sort of looking to make sure

00:04:08.375 --> 00:04:09.833
that the shoe fits the foot.

00:04:09.833 --> 00:04:11.666
This doesn't hurt the horse's foot at all.

00:04:11.666 --> 00:04:14.541
If you hold it on there for a long time,

00:04:14.541 --> 00:04:16.208
then the sensation of the heat

00:04:16.208 --> 00:04:17.625
will travel up the foot.

00:04:17.625 --> 00:04:19.875
They will eventually feel it, but I'd say

00:04:19.875 --> 00:04:22.875
five, six seconds is
probably all you need.

00:04:24.166 --> 00:04:26.208
And then this will make a mirror image

00:04:26.208 --> 00:04:28.250
from the shoe to the foot surface.

00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:29.458
So as you burn it on,

00:04:29.458 --> 00:04:31.791
it makes it perfectly flat.

00:04:31.791 --> 00:04:33.791
So it's a perfect snug fit.

00:04:39.625 --> 00:04:40.791
Once they're fitted

00:04:40.791 --> 00:04:42.875
and they're the right
shape for the horse needs,

00:04:42.875 --> 00:04:43.958
you would need to cool them down.

00:04:43.958 --> 00:04:46.125
So a bucket of water is
a good old-fashioned way

00:04:46.125 --> 00:04:48.125
of getting it down to
handling temperature,

00:04:48.125 --> 00:04:49.083
so you can pick it up

00:04:49.083 --> 00:04:50.875
and use it with your own hands.

00:04:52.875 --> 00:04:55.333
The nails probably only go about

00:04:55.333 --> 00:04:57.541
2 or 3 mil into the hoof wall,

00:04:57.541 --> 00:04:59.208
and then they'll go up the hoof wall

00:04:59.208 --> 00:05:00.708
and then come out the side.

00:05:00.708 --> 00:05:02.833
So as you nail it in, it curves out.

00:05:02.833 --> 00:05:04.583
So then you can flick it over

00:05:04.583 --> 00:05:06.583
and clamp it onto the foot.

00:05:09.541 --> 00:05:11.208
And I'm using my clenching tongs.

00:05:11.208 --> 00:05:13.625
These actually flick
over the end of the nail

00:05:13.625 --> 00:05:14.833
and push it back into the foot,

00:05:14.833 --> 00:05:16.500
so it grips and stays on

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:18.375
to create what is known as clenches.

00:05:18.375 --> 00:05:20.375
And then I would use a rasp,

00:05:20.375 --> 00:05:22.208
and that would buff the foot up,

00:05:22.208 --> 00:05:23.333
make it all nice and smooth,

00:05:23.333 --> 00:05:24.875
'cause you don't want any sharp edges

00:05:24.875 --> 00:05:26.500
for the horse to catch itself.

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:28.125
So this is the finishing process

00:05:28.125 --> 00:05:29.916
to make it all basically nice and pretty,

00:05:29.916 --> 00:05:32.291
and what people would consider as part

00:05:32.291 --> 00:05:34.333
of getting their nails done, really.

00:05:35.208 --> 00:05:37.375
As you file the hoof wall off,

00:05:37.375 --> 00:05:38.916
you're actually taking off a very, very

00:05:38.916 --> 00:05:40.875
fine layer off the outside,

00:05:40.875 --> 00:05:42.916
which maintains the moisture in the feet,

00:05:42.916 --> 00:05:44.125
and it's like a protective layer.

00:05:44.125 --> 00:05:46.333
So you would replenish this with a grease

00:05:46.333 --> 00:05:49.000
or any oil or anything shiny, really.

00:05:52.166 --> 00:05:54.416
If you're malnourished
or anything like that,

00:05:54.416 --> 00:05:56.500
fingernails and hair are the first thing

00:05:56.500 --> 00:05:57.416
that would show that.

00:05:57.416 --> 00:05:58.791
Same as any animals, you know,

00:05:58.791 --> 00:06:00.125
with dogs, cats, or anything like that.

00:06:00.125 --> 00:06:02.500
Hygiene is a big factor.

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:04.416
If you've got poor hygiene of a horse,

00:06:04.416 --> 00:06:06.291
you know, in a stable
that's not cleaned out,

00:06:06.291 --> 00:06:08.458
poor maintenance of its feet,

00:06:08.458 --> 00:06:10.375
not being trimmed or looked after,

00:06:10.375 --> 00:06:12.666
will eventually affect
a horse or any animal.

00:06:12.666 --> 00:06:13.958
In the wild, horses are not actually

00:06:13.958 --> 00:06:16.291
meant to live as long as
we allow them to live.

00:06:16.291 --> 00:06:18.166
Normally, as they get to an age of,

00:06:18.166 --> 00:06:20.083
you know, it could be 10 to 15,

00:06:20.083 --> 00:06:22.166
depending on type, breed, environment,

00:06:22.166 --> 00:06:24.041
and the predators that
they would naturally have,

00:06:24.041 --> 00:06:25.208
they would slow down,

00:06:25.208 --> 00:06:26.375
and then the predators would get them.

00:06:26.375 --> 00:06:28.666
So we have them in such good pasture

00:06:28.666 --> 00:06:29.958
and maintain them so well,

00:06:29.958 --> 00:06:31.541
they live actually a lot longer.

